I wrote a little app called "clip" that pastes its args to the clipboard. It turns out to be very handy. For example it's a nice way to get a file name from my DOS box into some other place, because DOS does arg completion, I can just type "clip f - tab" and get the name.
The other big place its been useful is copying command lines to the MSVC debugger property sheet, and turning command lines into batch files.
Clip is obviously trivial, the entire code is :
void CopyStringToClipboard(const char * str);
int main(int argc,const char *argv[])
    String out;
    
    for(int argi=1;argi < argc;argi++)
        if ( argi > 1 ) out += " ";
        out += argv[argi];
    
    lprintf("clip : \"%s\"\n",out);
    
    CopyStringToClipboard(out.CStr());
                
    return 0;
void CopyStringToClipboard(const char * str)
    // test to see if we can open the clipboard first before
    // wasting any cycles with the memory allocation
    if ( ! OpenClipboard(NULL))
        return;
        
    // Empty the Clipboard. This also has the effect
    // of allowing Windows to free the memory associated
    // with any data that is in the Clipboard
    EmptyClipboard();
    // Ok. We have the Clipboard locked and it's empty. 
    // Now let's allocate the global memory for our data.
    // Here I'm simply using the GlobalAlloc function to 
    // allocate a block of data equal to the text in the
    // "to clipboard" edit control plus one character for the
    // terminating null character required when sending
    // ANSI text to the Clipboard.
    HGLOBAL hClipboardData;
    hClipboardData = GlobalAlloc(GMEM_DDESHARE,strlen(str)+1);
    // Calling GlobalLock returns to me a pointer to the 
    // data associated with the handle returned from 
    char * pchData;
    pchData = (char*)GlobalLock(hClipboardData);
            
    // At this point, all I need to do is use the standard 
    // C/C++ strcpy function to copy the data from the local 
    // variable to the global memory.
    strcpy(pchData, str);
            
    // Once done, I unlock the memory - remember you 
    // don't call GlobalFree because Windows will free the 
    // memory automatically when EmptyClipboard is next 
    // called. 
    GlobalUnlock(hClipboardData);
            
    // Now, set the Clipboard data by specifying that 
    // ANSI text is being used and passing the handle to
    // the global memory.
    SetClipboardData(CF_TEXT,hClipboardData);
            
    // Finally, when finished I simply close the Clipboard
    // which has the effect of unlocking it so that other
    // applications can examine or modify its contents.
    CloseClipboard();
(BTW note that the lprintf of my string class in main is not a bug - that's an autoprintf which handles everything magically
and fantastically)
(I didn't remember where I got that clipboard code, but a quick Google indicates it came from Tom Archer at CodeProject )

A Beginner’s Guide To Real Estate Fund Of Funds (FoF)
Aug 21, 2025
Are you intrigued by the potential of real estate investing but hesitant about the complexities of direct ownership? Enter the real estate fund of funds (FoF), a unique investment vehicle offering instant diversification and professional management within the exciting world of real estate. Dive deeper and discover how this innovative approach can help you unlock the real estate market's potential without the hassle.
If you’re serious about doing your first real estate deal, don’t waste time guessing what works. Our FREE Training walks you through how to consistently find deals, flip houses, and build passive income—without expensive marketing or trial and error.
This FREE Training gives you the same system our students use to start fast and scale smart. Watch it today—so you can stop wondering and start closing.
While investing in a real estate fund of funds can offer benefits like diversification and professional management, it's essential to understand that they present a unique investment structure with specific regulations; this warrants a deeper discussion into precisely what a fund of funds is, including the specialized realm of fund of funds real estate.
As their names suggest, FoFs essentially act as funds that invest in other funds. This layered approach can provide both opportunities and complexities that set them apart from traditional investments. As a result, governmental regulations are tailored to FOFs to ensure transparency and investor protection. Consequently, the more you know about real estate funds of funds, the better, and we are here to tell you.
This article aims to unravel the nuances of FoFs. We'll explore what is a fund of funds, dissect their pros and cons, and discuss the specific considerations for fund of funds real estate. By the end, you'll be equipped to decide whether this investment vehicle aligns with your goals and risk tolerance.
*Before we begin our guide on real estate fund of funds, we invite you to view our video on How To Get Into Real Estate With No Money! Host and CEO of Real Estate Skills, Alex Martinez, provides the perfect guide for beginners to invest in real estate using no capital of their own!
What Are Real Estate Fund Of Funds?
Within the diverse landscape of investment options, real estate fund of funds (FoFs) stand out for their unique structure and potential benefits.
While fund of funds are not exclusive to real estate, this specific iteration offers a compelling approach for real estate investors seeking exposure to the real estate market without the complexities of direct ownership.
Unlike traditional investments in individual properties, real estate FoFs pool investor capital and strategically allocate it across a portfolio of existing real estate funds. These underlying funds can represent various property types, geographic locations, and investment strategies.
Due to their unique makeup, real estate fund of funds can be particularly attractive to investors seeking:
- Exposure to the real estate market without the burden of direct property ownership and management (similar to real estate investment trusts (REITs)).
- Diversification within their existing real estate assets to manage risk and volatility.
- Professional, diversified guidance and expert selection of underlying real estate investments.
It's crucial to remember that the layered structure of FoFs comes with its own set of fees and potential complexities. Therefore, a thorough evaluation of the specific FoF, its investment strategies, and associated fees is essential before making any investment decisions.
*For in-depth training on real estate investing, Real Estate Skills offers extensive courses to get you ready to make your first investment! Attend our FREE training and gain insider knowledge, expert strategies, and essential skills to make the most of every real estate opportunity that comes your way!
How Does A Fund Of Funds Work In Real Estate?
Let's imagine you're interested in investing in real estate, but buying and managing properties directly seems daunting. A real estate fund of funds offers an alternative route that can be simplified into these steps:
- Choosing An FoF: You research and select a real estate FoF that aligns with your investment goals and risk tolerance. Consider factors like the FoF's focus (residential, commercial, etc.), historical performance, and fees.
- Investing: You invest a certain amount of money into the chosen FoF through a brokerage, joining a pool of other investors.
- Management's Expertise: The FoF's fund manager team, comprised of real estate experts, takes charge of your investment. They carefully select a portfolio of existing real estate funds to invest in.
- Diversification: Your alternative investment is now spread across numerous underlying real estate funds (not unlike real estate mutual funds and ETFs); this means you indirectly gain exposure to various real estate properties, locations, and investment strategies.
- Returns: The track record of the underlying real estate funds ultimately determines the returns generated by the FoF. You'll receive potential income based on your proportional share in the FoF.
Essentially, a real estate FoF allows you to participate in the real estate market without directly owning properties. The FoF handles the complexities of choosing and managing real estate investments, offering the benefits of diversification and professional expertise.
Read Also: How To Invest In Commercial Real Estate: A Beginner's Guide
Benefits Of Real Estate Fund Of Funds
The benefits of real estate fund of funds include, but are not limited to:
- Instant Diversification: One of the primary advantages of a real estate FoF is its instant diversification. Your investment is automatically spread across multiple underlying real estate funds, potentially covering different property types, locations, and investment strategies. This diversification helps mitigate risk and reduces your reliance on the performance of any single property or market.
- Professional Management: Real estate FoFs are managed by experienced real estate professionals with extensive market knowledge and investment expertise. They handle selecting, monitoring, and rebalancing the underlying real estate funds, saving you valuable time and effort.
- Accessibility: Some institutional-grade real estate funds have high minimum investment requirements, making them inaccessible to individual investors. A FoF can provide a gateway to these exclusive opportunities, allowing you to participate in potentially high-performing real estate deals that may otherwise be out of reach.
- Potential For Stable Returns: Real estate investments have the potential to generate income through rental payments and property appreciation. By investing in a well-managed real estate FoF, you can indirectly tap into these potential income streams and participate in the long-term growth of the real estate sector.
Drawbacks Of Real Estate Fund Of Funds
The drawbacks of real estate fund of funds include, but are not limited to:
- Higher Fees: One of the most significant drawbacks of real estate FoFs is the layered fee structure. You'll pay management fees for the FoF itself and the fees associated with each underlying real estate fund. These multiple layers of fees can eat into your overall returns.
- Reduced Control: When you invest in a FoF, you have less direct control over the specific properties and investments held within the underlying funds. The FoF manager ultimately makes the investment decisions, limiting your ability to customize your portfolio.
- Potential For Underperformance: Even with professional management, there's always the risk that the underlying real estate funds selected by the FoF might underperform; this could result in lower returns or even losses for investors in the FoF.
- Illiquidity: Real estate investments, in general, are considered relatively illiquid, meaning they cannot be easily bought or sold on short notice. This lack of liquidity is further compounded in a real estate FoF, as it takes additional time to potentially sell your shares in the FoF and the underlying funds.
How To Invest In A Real Estate Fund Of Funds (5 Steps)
Entering the world of real estate investing can be exciting, but navigating the complexities can feel daunting. If you're a new investor considering a real estate fund of funds, understanding the process can empower you to make informed decisions. Here's a breakdown of the five key steps involved:
- Research & Education: Before diving in, it's vital to educate yourself. Understand the concept of "what is a fund of funds" and how real estate FoFs function. Explore their benefits, potential drawbacks, and how they differ from directly investing in real estate or individual funds. Research available FoFs in the market, paying attention to their focus areas (e.g., residential or commercial real estate), historical performance, fee fund structures, and the expertise of their management teams. Resources like prospectuses, financial advisor consultations, and online research can be helpful tools in this stage.
- Define Your Financial Goals: Investing without clear goals is like driving without a destination. Take time to assess your risk tolerance. How comfortable are you with potential ups and downs in the market? Determine your investment time horizon. Are you looking to build wealth over the long term or seeking short-term gains? Finally, establish your target returns. What level of return are you hoping to achieve with your investment? Aligning your goals with the right FoF requires clear self-awareness.
- Seek Professional Support: While not always necessary, seeking professional guidance can be invaluable. A financial advisor or planner can assess your overall financial situation, recommend suitable FoFs based on your goals, and help you integrate this investment into your portfolio. Their expertise can provide valuable insights and ensure alignment with your broader financial strategy.
- Fulfill Legal & Financial Prerequisites: Ensure you meet any legal and financial requirements before investing. Some FoFs might have specific eligibility criteria, such as being an accredited investor or meeting minimum investment thresholds. Additionally, gather necessary documentation, including proof of identity, income verification, and investment suitability forms. Fulfilling these prerequisites ensures you're eligible and prepared to invest.
- Deposit Funds & Invest: Once your eligibility is confirmed, follow the guidelines the chosen FoF provides to deposit funds into your investment account. Based on their established strategy, the FoF management team will use your deposited funds to invest in the selected real estate funds. Remember, due diligence is crucial throughout the entire process.
Exiting a real estate fund of funds typically involves submitting a redemption request to the FoF. The FoF's governing documents will outline specific procedures, timelines, and any potential redemption fees for withdrawing your investment. Be aware that real estate investments are often illiquid, meaning it may take some time to receive your distributions relative to your allocation after initiating the redemption process.
Syndication Vs. Fund Of Funds In Real Estate
While both provide indirect access to the real estate market, real estate syndications and real estate fund of funds differ in their structure and offer distinct advantages and considerations for investors.
Real estate syndications pool capital from multiple investors to purchase, manage, and sell a specific property or portfolio. Investors become direct owners of the underlying real estate companies, sharing in the profits, losses, and associated tax benefits; this offers greater potential control and transparency over the investment but also requires more due diligence and participation in decision-making. Additionally, syndications tend to have higher minimum investment requirements and are generally less liquid, meaning it can be harder to sell your investment quickly.
Real estate FoFs, on the other hand, invest in a portfolio of existing real estate funds instead of individual properties. This layered structure provides instant diversification across various property types, locations, and investment strategies. Investors don't directly own the underlying assets but benefit from the expertise and risk management of the FoF's management team. This approach offers convenience, lower minimum investment amounts, and potentially greater liquidity. However, FoFs typically have higher fees due to the layered structure and less control over the specific investments held within the underlying real estate investment funds.
Choosing between a real estate syndication and FoF ultimately boils down to your individual investment goals and preferences. A syndication might be suitable if you seek deeper involvement, control, and potentially higher returns. A FoF could be better if you prioritize convenience, diversification, and professional management.
Final Thoughts On Real Estate Fund Of Funds
Real estate funds of funds offer a compelling path to building wealth through real estate investments without the burden of direct ownership. These vehicles democratize the industry, but it's crucial to remember their complex structures, fees, and potential risks. Before diving into a real estate fund of funds, careful research, and due diligence are essential to ensure this investment path aligns with your financial goals and risk tolerance.
*Disclosure: Real Estate Skills is not a law firm, and the information contained here does not constitute legal advice. You should consult with an attorney before making any legal conclusions. The information presented here is educational in nature. All investments involve risks, and the past performance of an investment, industry, sector, and/or market does not guarantee future returns or results. Investors are responsible for any investment decision they make. Such decisions should be based on an evaluation of their financial situation, investment objectives, risk tolerance, and liquidity needs.

Klau Geomatics is now launching MakeItAccurate, a revolutionary new global GNSS data correction and processing service.
MakeItAccurate takes data from any GNSS receiver on your drone or survey equipment and makes it accurate. You can now achieve cm level accuracy without the need for base stations, RTK links, CORS data or any other external inputs. Just the data from your drone.
How accurate is MakeItAccurate?
MakeItAccurate requires only the raw GNSS data from your drone to produce a highly precise trajectory and turn the traditional autonomous 3-5m GPS accuracy to 3-5cm anywhere in the world.
In many parts of the USA, Europe, Japan, Australia and New Zealand, absolute accuracy of 2-3cm XYZ will be achieved. In these areas, the KlauPPK processing engine applies sophisticated hybrid PPK/PPP algorithms, merging global PPP clock and orbit corrections with many distant CORS stations to achieve this very high absolute accuracy.
Why use MakeItAccurate?
This solution enables enterprise drone operations to achieve very high accuracy across their entire global operations with one simple repeatable workflow.
Sectors such as insurance, telco’s and utilities can scale their operations without additional survey expertise and site-specific data constraints.
The same process works for multiple operators on thousands of sites. Every site is the same, one simple solution: just fly your drone, anywhere. Achieve consistent, very-high accuracy every time.
Who can use MakeItAccurate?
MakeItAccurate supports data from all GNSS manufacturers. Native support for DJI M210v2RTK or Phantom4RTK drones returns the precise camera positions with centimetre level accuracy. Other drones using external PPK GNSS products can also achieve highly accurate kinematic trajectories and camera coordinates.
A MakeItAccurate API is available to push raw GNSS data to the processing engine and return highly accurate coordinates, with full reporting on the accuracy achieved for the entire trajectory or each camera event.
GNSS hardware manufacturers can offer a custom service to value add to their products. Software developers offering artificial intelligence technology, photogrammetry processing or any other outcome that benefits from high accuracy can use the MakeItAccurate API. SaaS platforms that offer photogrammetry processing and visualization can now offer a simple checkbox to “Make It Accurate”.
