Frequently asked questions
Yes. French citizens need a visa for Puerto Rico, and the United States ESTA can be applied for online.
$62.27. That is $40.27 in government fees and $22.00 for our service.
From 13 hours. Governments do not guarantee processing times, so apply as early as you can.
Up to 90 days per entry.
You must enter Puerto Rico within 2 years of approval. That window is separate from how long you may stay.
Multiple entry — you may leave and return while it is valid.
Approval takes from 13 hours, and you then have 2 years to enter Puerto Rico. You can apply shortly before you fly — just not so early that the entry window runs out.
Still have a question?
Ask our assistant. It answers in the chat, and we hand it the route you are looking at — French passport, travelling to Puerto Rico — so you do not have to repeat it.
What Puerto Rico may ask for
Not every traveller is asked for all of these. A valid passport is checked every time; the rest is a spot check at the border, and some items belong to a visa application rather than the crossing itself.
- Valid US visa or ESTA for international travelers
- US citizens need only government-issued photo ID
- No customs for domestic US flights to Puerto Rico
- US Dollar is the official currency - no exchange needed
- US health insurance typically covers Puerto Rico
- International visitors clear US immigration at first US entry point
Entry requirements for French travellers
These rules apply specifically to French passport holders entering Puerto Rico as tourists. Each one links to the official source where available.
- Required Electronic Travel Authorization (ESTA) Travelers need a travel authorization to visit the Puerto Rico for Business or Tourism. The Electronic System for Travel Authorization (ESTA) is an online application. Once approved, travelers receive their ESTA confirmation electronically. 
  - Travelers visiting for more than 90 days per entry require a Visitor visa. An ESTA is electronically linked to a passport and valid for 2 years. If travelers receive a new passport, they must apply for a new ESTA. United States permanent residents or holders of valid United States visas don’t need an ESTA. Travelers who visited Cuba on or after January 12, 2021, are no longer eligible for an ESTA and must apply for a visitor visa to enter the United States. Travelers who visited Iran, Iraq, North Korea, Sudan, Syria, Libya, Somalia and Yemen on or after March 1, 2011, are no longer eligible for an ESTA and must apply for a visitor visa to enter the United States. Different requirements may apply to travelers who have visited these countries for diplomatic or military purposes. For further details on exceptions, please review the government website.
United States Department of State Last checked 2026-07-28
- Required Travelers require a passport valid for the duration of stay in United States Travelers need a valid passport to enter United States. The traveler's passport must remain valid throughout the traveler's stay in United States. The passport must have at least 1 blank page. 
  - Venezuelan passport holders will have a validity period of ten years from the expiration date of their passport and have valid admission to the United States, as long as the traveler has a valid visa. Venezuelan nationals who are not in possession of a passport, or whose passport has not been rendered valid by the decree, may be able to travel with a visa attached to a Form DS-232.
US Customs and Border Protection Last checked 2026-07-28
- Not required Proof of COVID-19 vaccination is not required for Puerto Rico Travelers don’t need proof of COVID-19 vaccination before traveling to Puerto Rico. Last checked 2026-07-28
- Not required Proof of pre-departure COVID-19 test is not required Travelers don’t need a COVID-19 test before traveling to Puerto Rico. Last checked 2026-07-28
