A Life Well Lived
 
Bill Garvin, a man of unwavering dedication and love, passed away surrounded by the memories he cherished. His legacy is woven into the fabric of our lives, and we celebrate the remarkable journey he undertook.
 
A Passion for Perfection
 
Bill’s meticulous nature extended beyond his yard and home—it permeated every aspect of his existence. Whether tending to his yard or maintaining his house, he found joy in perfecting the details. His pride in these accomplishments was evident to all who knew him.
 
The Great Outdoors
 
In his youth, Bill reveled in the rugged beauty of Iron Mountain. Hunting and fishing were not mere pastimes; they were sacred rituals. Bill shared these traditions with his children, passing down not only skills but also a deep love for nature. His grandkids, wide-eyed and eager, rode alongside him on his ATV through the serene Mountain Meadows.
 
The Collector’s Heart
 
Cars held a special place in Bill’s heart. Over the years, he amassed a collection that reflected his passion for craftsmanship and design. Each vehicle told a story—a testament to his appreciation for the artistry of the road.
 
 
Bill’s impact extended beyond his family. As the owner of G&G Auto Parts, he built lasting friendships within the Cheyenne community. His commitment to service and camaraderie left an indelible mark. Later, he transitioned to Laramie County Schools District, where he continued to contribute, leaving behind a legacy of dedication.
 
 
Bill’s heart swelled with pride for his grandchildren. Their laughter echoed through his home, and their achievements were his own. He reveled in their milestones. His love knew no bounds.
 
 
Bill Garvin leaves behind a tapestry of memories—of starlit nights, roaring engines, and shared laughter. As he reunites with loved ones who preceded him, we honor his spirit. May the winds carry his legacy, and may the northern lights dance in celebration of a life well-lived.
 
Bill is preceded in death by his grandparents, parents Bill and Betty Garvin, his sister, Sandy Lee, a nephew, Brian Lee, a grandson, Cameron Sharpe and many aunts and uncle’s who he cherished.
 
He is survived by his wife, Linda, his three daughters, Tana (partner Dave), Dawn Garvin, and Kimi La Rosa (Lisa), his 6 grand children, Tyler, Justin, Bryce, Ryan, Kelsey and Regan and 4 great grandchildren, Ashtyn, Aaliyah, Paisley and Addison.

Positive Parenting Strategies for Challenging Behaviors
2 min read
Every child has moments of frustration, disappointment, and challenging behavior. Tantrums, refusing directions, arguing, hitting, biting, or difficulty managing emotions are common as children develop self-control and communication skills. Positive parenting helps children learn appropriate behaviors while strengthening the parent-child relationship.
Behavior is a form of communication. Before responding, ask yourself, ‘What is my child trying to tell me?’ Children may be tired, hungry, overwhelmed, anxious, frustrated, or unable to express their needs. Children with developmental delays, autism spectrum disorder, ADHD, language disorders, or sensory processing differences may need additional support communicating their feelings.
One of the most effective strategies is building a strong relationship with your child. Spend time reading, playing, talking, or enjoying activities together each day. Children who feel connected are often more willing to cooperate and follow directions.
Children thrive with predictable routines and clear expectations. Consistent schedules, simple directions, visual supports, and timers help children understand what is expected. Breaking tasks into small steps often improves success.
Positive reinforcement is one of the most powerful behavior strategies. Notice and praise appropriate behavior using specific comments such as, ‘I like how you shared,’ or ‘Thank you for listening the first time.’ Sticker charts, extra playtime, and other meaningful rewards may also encourage positive behavior.
When challenging behavior occurs, remain calm. Children learn emotional regulation by watching adults. Set clear limits, avoid long lectures during emotional moments, and discuss better choices after everyone has calmed down. Teach replacement skills such as asking for help, using words to express feelings, taking deep breaths, or requesting a break.
Set realistic expectations based on your child’s developmental level. Celebrate progress rather than perfection. If behaviors become severe, frequent, or interfere with daily life, seek guidance from your pediatrician or qualified professionals such as psychologists, occupational therapists, behavior specialists, or speech-language pathologists.
Remember that every child is learning. Challenging behaviors do not define your child or your parenting. With patience, consistency, encouragement, and positive guidance, children can develop the self-control, communication, and problem-solving skills they need to succeed at home, school, and in the community.

