Cambodia’s Premium Cashew Sector Targeting China
At the 2026 CFNA China International Tree Nuts Conference, which took place from Aug. 5 to 8 in Yiwu, An Dara, president of the Cambodia Cashew Federation, shared the latest data and national development strategy for Cambodia’s cashew industry. As a globally significant cashew producer, Cambodia is currently trying to transition from a raw material supplier to a high-value processing hub, with China identified as its most important strategic market for the future.
The Cambodia Cashew Federation is a government-led organization mandated to represent the national cashew industry. Its core objective is to integrate the entire value chain, including growers, processors and exporters, under a single umbrella to formulate and implement national cashew policy, while also providing full-chain financial support to its members. The federation encompasses 342 cashew communities, connecting over 200,000 smallholder farmer families and 48 cashew processing facilities.
Although Cambodia is a major cashew producer, its domestic processing rate accounts for only about 5% of total production. The country’s limited domestic processing capacity means that over 90% of raw cashew nuts are exported to neighboring Vietnam for processing into higher-value products that are then sent on to the target export markets. To address this, the Cambodian government has set clear targets: to increase local processing to 25% by 2027, with an ambition to reach 50% by 2032.
Cambodian cashews are of excellent quality with 70% being the M23 variety, used to produce WW160 and WW180 kernels, the largest available on the global market. Chinese consumers show strong demand for high-quality cashews with large kernels, and Cambodian cashews, known for their large size, white color, sweet taste and rich nutritional content, are highly aligned with Chinese market preferences.
By the end of June 2026, Cambodia’s raw cashew exports had reached approximately 1.03 million metric tons, a year-on-year increase of 28%. Despite global market challenges such as tightening supply, Cambodia’s position as a leading raw cashew supplier remains solid.
Image: 2026 CFNA China International Tree Nuts Conference

My people, wetin dey happen na? Bayer Leverkusen don show their class as dem scatter St. Pauli 3-0 for DFB Pokal quarter-final wey dem play for BayArena. Na big game wey Leverkusen use pepper soup style take waka go semi-final.
First goal come from Martin Terrier for 32nd minute. Na sweet volley wey dey make fans dey shout ‘Pele!’ for their house. But no be only Terrier carry the team, Patrik Schick join the party for 63rd minute, as he take volley from the far post wey make goalkeeper no fit answer.
But wait, make I yarn you the better gist: as dey dey think sey St. Pauli fit try come back, na injury con gree dem. Jones wey just enter the field limped out after 35 minutes. E be like sey him leg dey play hide and seek.
As the match dey waka go stoppage time, Jonas Hofmann come add one more for the scoreboard. He collect better pass and slot am inside net, e no wan hear word. Na so Bayer Leverkusen finish dem with wetin dem call ‘convincing victory’.
Dis win na dem third straight time wey Bayer dey enter DFB Pokal semi-final, and dem still dey hope say dis season go be their year again after dem win am for 2023/24 season. St. Pauli, sha, dey struggle for Bundesliga, dem win only one match for their last eight. Na wetin dem dey call ‘serious wahala’.
Leverkusen dey show say dem serious for dis cup matter, as dem don dey chop wins like say na rice and beans. And how dem dey manage all dis? Na because dem fit keep their focus no matter wetin the other teams dey do.
Next match na for semi-final, make we see if dem go fit take the cup again. Dem dey on fire, and as you sabi, dem boys go want show firework. Na only time go tell!

