The Impact of Charitable Gaming in the Community - St. Thomas Elgin General Hospital
The St. Thomas Elgin General Hospital (STEGH) Auxiliary is a volunteer driven organization that exists to support patient care equipment and capital renovations at the St. Thomas Elgin General Hospital through fundraising.
The opportunity to participate at Jackpot Time St. Thomas has given the STEGH Auxiliary a tremendous means of raising a significant amount of funds and replacing some traditional fundraising opportunities that were challenged and/or discontinued during Covid.
The STEGH Auxiliary’s most recent pledge to the Hospital is directed to the Transforming Tomorrow capital campaign, an $8 million investment in STEGH’s diagnostic imaging department which includes an MRI.
Support of this exciting project will help the hospital provide regional level care in St. Thomas. Currently, 5,000 patients must travel outside the County annually for MRI scans. With the installation of a long-awaited MRI as well as renovations and new medical equipment for x-ray, ultrasound, nuclear medicine and CT, families, friends and neighbours will be able to access the level of high quality care they need and deserve, close to home.

Marketing can amplify your reach and bookings when you build a targeted list, craft compelling welcome sequences, and segment subscribers to deliver personalized trip ideas and offers. You should optimize subject lines, include clear calls to action, and use analytics to refine what converts. Start with simple opt-ins and test formats – see 10 Email Opt-In Ideas for Outdoor Travel Bloggers for practical prompts to grow your list.
Key Takeaways:
- Offer targeted lead magnets (packing lists, city guides, itineraries) to grow a quality, interested subscriber base.
- Segment lists by destination, travel style, and booking intent to deliver personalized, relevant content.
- Use storytelling and compelling subject lines/preview text to boost opens and build trust with readers.
- Automate workflows (welcome series, trip reminders, abandoned-booking nudges) and include clear mobile-friendly CTAs.
- Track opens, CTR, conversions, and unsubscribe rates; A/B test content and maintain list hygiene and compliance.
Understanding the Importance of Email Marketing
Because you control deliverability, email remains the highest-return channel for growing travel revenue: industry data often cites roughly $36 back for every $1 spent and average open rates in the 20-25% range for engaged lists. Use automated welcome sequences, destination-based segmentation and timed offers to keep traffic steady when social algorithms fluctuate, and track revenue per subscriber to prove impact on bookings and affiliate income.
Benefits for Travel Bloggers
You can turn readers into repeat customers, increasing direct bookings, affiliate conversions and sponsored campaign value. Email subscribers typically convert at higher rates than social visitors-often 2-5% versus under 1%-and a focused campaign can drive a booking spike during shoulder seasons. Offer exclusive itineraries, early-bird deals or partner discounts to lift lifetime value and make your audience more attractive to brands.
Building a Loyal Audience
You grow loyalty by delivering value immediately: deploy a 3-email welcome series in the first week, segment by intent or destination, and send consistent, personalized content (1-2 emails weekly or campaign-focused bursts). Use storytelling, local tips and clear CTAs to convert interest into action; loyal subscribers will open, click and share more often, becoming repeat bookers and reliable traffic sources for six months to a year and beyond.
Focus on segmentation and automation to deepen loyalty: split lists by trip type (adventure, family, luxury), destination and booking timeline, then send tailored sequences-Day 0 welcome, Day 2 local guide, Day 7 curated deals. Test subject lines and send times, measure opens, CTR and revenue per subscriber, and treat a 30-40% open rate and 2-4% CTR as realistic benchmarks while iterating content based on what converts.
Building Your Email List
Target high-intent spots on your site: exit-intent popups, hero banners, and dedicated landing pages convert best, often yielding 2-5% opt-in rates. Use social proof (subscriber counts, testimonials) and clear benefits to push conversions. You should A/B test copy and CTAs weekly, and set measurable goals-e.g., add 500 subscribers/month or increase welcome-series open rate to 40%-so growth becomes predictable and scalable.
Techniques for List Growth
Use content upgrades (checklists, packing lists) inside relevant posts to boost opt-ins by up to 3x; run targeted Facebook/Instagram ads at $0.30-$1 per lead to scale quickly. Partner with complementary travel creators for joint newsletters, host giveaways requiring email entry, and implement a referral program that rewards sharing-these tactics combined often accelerate growth from hundreds to thousands in months.
Lead Magnets and Incentives
Offer high-value, immediately usable items: a 7-day city itinerary, printable packing checklist, exclusive discount codes (10-20%), or an email-only mini-course. You should match the magnet to the blog post-e.g., beach packing list in a beach guide-to lift relevance and conversion, and promote it across social and your homepage.
Design magnets as short, practical assets-3-10 page PDFs, a 5-email mini-course, or a 2-video series-so you can produce them quickly and test. Deliver instantly via automated email and follow with a 3-email welcome series over 7 days to onboard subscribers. Choose single vs double opt-in based on quality: double opt-in typically cuts list size 10-30% but often boosts open rates 15-40% and reduces spam complaints. Test which magnet (itinerary vs discount) yields the highest lifetime value per subscriber.
Crafting Compelling Email Content
Types of Content That Converts
You should balance timely offers with evergreen value-mix weekly digests, destination guides, trip reports, and flash deals to sustain opens and bookings; industry benchmarks show engaged travel lists can hit 10-20% click rates on targeted campaigns. Use 30-50 character subject lines and A/B test CTAs to measure lift, testing 2-3 formats each month to find winners. Recognizing which formats drive bookings lets you focus resources on the highest-ROI content.
- Weekly newsletter: curated stories + 1 CTA
- Long-form destination guide: deep value, SEO-friendly
- Trip report/case study: social proof + itinerary
- Deals & flash sales: urgency-driven conversions
- Local tips/mini-itineraries: high engagement for repeat readers
| Newsletter | Curated content + 1 conversion link; good for retention | 
| Destination Guide | Evergreen asset that drives opens and long-term traffic | 
| Trip Report | Real-world proof that converts readers into bookers | 
| Deals / Flash Sales | Short windows increase CTR and immediate revenue | 
| Local Tips / Itineraries | High click intent for planning-stage subscribers | 
You can start by segmenting on intent, past bookings, geography, and travel style, using 3-5 core segments to keep workflows manageable; combine merge tags (first name, last destination) with dynamic content blocks to boost relevance and open rates by roughly mid-double digits compared to generic sends.
Implement behavioral triggers: an abandoned-booking flow at 1 hour, 24 hours, and 72 hours with escalating incentives; a pre-trip series 7, 3, and 1 day before travel containing packing tips and local add-ons; and reactivation campaigns for lapsed subscribers after 90 days. Use analytics to track CTR, conversion rate, and revenue per recipient, and iterate-A/B test subject lines, images, and the number of dynamic blocks to find the combinations that move bookings most efficiently.
Designing Effective Email Campaigns
You should use modular templates at a fixed width (600-680px) with clear hierarchy: 22-28px headlines, 14-16px body text and one to two CTAs per message. Keep subject lines to ~40-50 characters and preheaders to 35-50 characters, A/B testing both; test send times (e.g., 8 AM vs 5 PM) to lift open rates. Also limit total HTML to under 102 KB to avoid Gmail clipping and optimize images to reduce load time.
Best Practices for Email Design
Prioritize scannability: short paragraphs, 40-60 characters per line, and 1.5 line height. Use high-contrast buttons sized for touch (minimum 44×44 px) and compress images under ~150-200 KB; include descriptive alt text. Maintain brand consistency with a 1-3 color CTA palette, single-column layouts for clarity, and run A/B tests on subject lines and CTA color to measure CTR improvements.
Since more than half of opens happen on mobile, design single-column layouts, use minimum 16px body type for legibility, and make CTAs full-width or large enough for thumb taps. Prioritize one clear action above the fold and keep load times low by compressing images and using system fonts.
Implement responsive CSS with media queries and inline styles for compatibility across clients; avoid relying on background images or hover effects. Serve retina-ready images with srcset, keep the first 50-60 characters of preheader impactful on narrow screens, and test extensively on iOS Mail, Gmail app, and Android devices using tools like Litmus or Email on Acid to catch rendering quirks.
Analyzing Email Marketing Performance
Key Metrics to Track
You should monitor open rate, click-through rate (CTR), click-to-open rate (CTOR), conversion rate, unsubscribe and bounce rates, plus list growth and revenue per email. Aim for 20-30% opens and 2-5% CTR in the travel niche; with 10,000 subscribers a 3% CTR means 300 clicks to your blog or affiliate offers. Segment-specific metrics often reveal higher engagement-VIP segments frequently double average CTRs.
A/B Testing Strategies
Test one variable at a time-subject line, preheader, CTA, send time, or hero image-to isolate impact. Use a test sample of 10-20% or at least several hundred recipients; then roll the winner to the remainder. For example, testing two subject lines on 1,000 leads could show opens rising from 18% to 24%, a 33% lift that translates directly to more clicks and bookings.
Start with a clear hypothesis (e.g., shorter subject lines increase opens), split evenly and run until results reach statistical significance or 24-72 hours depending on list size. Keep tests isolated-don’t change subject line and CTA at once. For small lists (<5k) run week-long tests or use larger samples; for 10k+ lists you can detect a 5% relative lift with a few hundred recipients per variant. Log results to build a library of winning formulas for subject lines, layouts, and CTAs.
Automating Your Email Marketing
Automation lets you scale personal touches without extra hours: set up 4-6 core flows-welcome, booking confirmation, pre-trip checklist, upsell, post-trip survey, and re-engagement-and watch consistent touchpoints drive bookings. Send the welcome within an hour, a pre-trip checklist 3-7 days before travel, and a post-trip survey 24-72 hours after return. You should map each flow to a clear KPI (open, CTR, booking rate) and review performance monthly to iterate subject lines, CTAs, and timing.
Setting Up Automated Campaigns
Start by mapping your subscriber journey and defining triggers: signup, booking, site browsing, or inactivity. Build a 3-email welcome (immediate, 48 hours, one week), a booking sequence (confirmation, upsell, reminder), and a 2-step re-engagement after 30 and 60 days. Use dynamic tags for itinerary, destination, or recent posts, and A/B test subject lines and send times to lift open rates by measurable percentages.
Tools to Consider
Choose a platform that matches your complexity: ConvertKit for simple creator funnels, Klaviyo for revenue attribution and advanced segmentation, ActiveCampaign for built-in CRM and automation depth, Mailchimp for easy templates and reporting, and Brevo/Sendinblue for low-cost high-volume sends. Prioritize native integrations with Stripe, Shopify, booking engines, and Zapier to stitch your systems together.
If you sell trips or affiliate packages, Klaviyo’s granular revenue tracking and Shopify/Stripe integrations help attribute sales; ActiveCampaign is better when you need lead scoring and multi-channel automations (email+SMS); ConvertKit speeds up creator workflows with simple tagging; Mailchimp fits smaller lists under ~$50/month; Brevo scales affordably for large sends. Plan budgets from $0-$100+/month as lists grow, and test migrations on a subset to avoid deliverability hiccups.
To wrap up
With these considerations, your email strategy can become a reliable revenue and engagement driver for your travel blog. Emphasize segmentation, personalization, compelling travel storytelling, automated funnels for timely offers, and analytics to refine subject lines and send times. Prioritize subscriber value and consent to build trust and repeat bookings.
Q: How do I build an quality email list as a travel blogger?
A: Building a high-quality list begins with clear, compelling opt-ins that match your blog content. Offer lead magnets such as destination-specific mini-guides, packing checklists, printable itineraries, exclusive discount codes, or a multi-day travel email course. Use targeted placement: end-of-post CTAs on relevant articles, content upgrades inside long guides, exit-intent popups for high-traffic pages, and dedicated landing pages promoted via social and paid ads. Promote signups in your Instagram bio, YouTube descriptions, and video endcards. Partner with complementary creators or local tourism boards for co-branded giveaways to attract niche subscribers. Collect minimal, useful data (email, destination interest, travel dates) to enable segmentation while keeping the sign-up flow frictionless. Comply with consent laws (GDPR, CAN-SPAM) and verify opt-in sources to protect deliverability and list quality.
Q: What types of email content work best for travel audiences?
A: A mix of inspirational and practical content performs well. Send destination spotlights and photo-led storytelling to spark wanderlust, then follow with actionable itineraries, local tips, suggested neighborhoods, and budget breakdowns to help planning. Share trip reports, behind-the-scenes updates, and short video clips to build rapport. Timely deal alerts, curated gear lists, and seasonal packing guides drive conversions. Use a predictable cadence: a regular newsletter for community and updates, triggered emails for new posts or downloads, and campaign bursts for promotions or launches. Include clear CTAs (book now, read full guide, save PDF) and mobile-optimized layouts with strong visual hierarchy to increase click-throughs.
Q: How should I segment and personalize emails for travel subscribers?
A: Segment by destination interests, travel style (solo, family, luxury, budget), timing (planning window, last-minute), engagement level, and past purchases or clicks. Use preference centers at signup so subscribers choose topics and frequency. Personalize subject lines and preview text with destination names or reader first names, and use dynamic content blocks to show relevant itineraries or offers based on segment. Implement behavior-triggered flows: welcome series, pre-trip checklists for booked readers, and re-engagement sequences for inactive subscribers. Track opens, clicks, and site behavior to refine segments and tailor follow-ups that increase relevance and conversions.
Q: How can I keep subscribers engaged and reduce unsubscribes?
A: Maintain relevance and predictable expectations. Let subscribers choose content types and frequency up front, then honor those preferences with a preference center and varied cadence options. Craft concise, benefit-driven subject lines and strong preview text to set accurate expectations. Optimize for mobile, speed up load times, and use prominent CTAs. Conduct regular A/B tests on subject lines, send times, and content formats. Clean your list periodically by removing consistently inactive addresses and run re-engagement campaigns offering exclusive content or a chance to update preferences. Ensure deliverability by authenticating your domain (SPF, DKIM), monitoring spam complaints, and keeping HTML clean and accessible.
Q: What are effective ways for travel bloggers to monetize an email list?
A: Monetization paths include affiliate promotions for hotels, tours, gear, and booking platforms; sponsored newsletters or dedicated partner sends; selling digital products like destination guides, e-books, and itineraries; paid newsletters or membership tiers with premium content; and launching small group trips or online courses. Use segmented offers to match products with subscriber intent and track performance with UTM parameters and affiliate links. Disclose paid promotions per advertising guidelines and maintain editorial trust by limiting overly promotional sends. Test pricing, placement, and frequency to optimize revenue while preserving engagement and brand integrity.

